WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.534 --> 00:00:02.935
-Kendall, thank you so much
for coming back to our show.

00:00:02.935 --> 00:00:04.637
-Yeah, thanks for having me.
-You look gorgeous, oh, my gosh.

00:00:04.637 --> 00:00:06.272
-This is so nice to be back.

00:00:06.272 --> 00:00:07.940
-Yeah, it feels good
to have an audience, right?

00:00:07.940 --> 00:00:09.975
-Yeah, it does.

00:00:09.975 --> 00:00:11.344
-I want to get
into some cool stuff here,

00:00:11.344 --> 00:00:13.479
but I want to say first,
your sister Kylie

00:00:13.479 --> 00:00:15.314
is expecting her second child.

00:00:15.314 --> 00:00:16.849
-She is, she is.
It's really exciting.

00:00:16.849 --> 00:00:18.017
-Congratulations.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:18.017 --> 00:00:20.453
-Thank you.
-How did you -- How did you --

00:00:20.453 --> 00:00:23.456
How'd you hear the news?
-She called me,

00:00:23.456 --> 00:00:25.558
and I answered the phone,
and she just had

00:00:25.558 --> 00:00:28.161
her little sonogram -- like,
a picture of her sonogram.

00:00:28.161 --> 00:00:30.829
And I was like, "Oh, my God."
I wasn't really shocked

00:00:30.829 --> 00:00:33.633
'cause I, like, felt like
it could happen someday soon,

00:00:33.633 --> 00:00:36.903
but I was excited.
I was very excited, and --

00:00:36.903 --> 00:00:38.337
It's just a blessing,
it's amazing.

00:00:38.337 --> 00:00:40.974
-Are you a good babysitter?
-I'm all right.

00:00:40.974 --> 00:00:43.776
I always want to be, like,
the cool aunt and, like, liked,

00:00:43.776 --> 00:00:45.811
I guess, by all of them.
-Yes! Yeah, me too.

00:00:45.811 --> 00:00:48.047
-There is 18 of them now,
going on 20.

00:00:48.047 --> 00:00:49.748
There's two on the way.
-Is that correct?

00:00:49.748 --> 00:00:52.385
-So, yeah, my brother Burt
has one on the way,

00:00:52.385 --> 00:00:54.521
and then, my sister, obviously,
just announced hers, so...

00:00:54.521 --> 00:00:55.855
-Oh, my gosh.
So you're pretty used to it.

00:00:55.855 --> 00:00:58.491
-I have a lot of them.
But it's fun.

00:00:58.491 --> 00:01:01.894
It's really fun.
I try and be chill.

00:01:01.894 --> 00:01:04.330
-Yeah. Does your boyfriend
help out at all?

00:01:04.330 --> 00:01:05.531
-He loves them.

00:01:05.531 --> 00:01:07.533
Him and Stormi
have an amazing relationship.

00:01:07.533 --> 00:01:08.935
She has
the biggest crush on him,

00:01:08.935 --> 00:01:10.569
and I'm, like,
jealous sometimes.

00:01:10.569 --> 00:01:11.971
I'm like, "Stop."
-Yeah, you're like,

00:01:11.971 --> 00:01:13.172
"Come on," yeah.
-I'm like, "Can you guys not?"

00:01:13.172 --> 00:01:14.907
-"I'm the cool one," yeah.
-Yeah.

00:01:14.907 --> 00:01:17.310
-Devin Booker
is on the Phoenix Suns.

00:01:17.310 --> 00:01:19.112
He's amazing.
Just won a gold medal.

00:01:19.112 --> 00:01:20.880
-He did, yeah.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:20.880 --> 00:01:23.816
It's awesome.

00:01:23.816 --> 00:01:26.052
-I know that we talked about it.
You were very sporty

00:01:26.052 --> 00:01:27.787
when you were growing up.
You played basketball.

00:01:27.787 --> 00:01:29.488
-I did in ele--
Er, yeah,

00:01:29.488 --> 00:01:30.890
middle school
I played basketball.

00:01:30.890 --> 00:01:33.526
-Do you guys ever have a little
one-on-one competition?

00:01:33.526 --> 00:01:37.497
-We do sometimes. He --
We actually were just playing.

00:01:37.497 --> 00:01:40.133
We have, like -- our friends
had, like, a pool setup.

00:01:40.133 --> 00:01:41.534
You know, the hoops
that are in the pool?

00:01:41.534 --> 00:01:43.236
-Oh, there you go.
-And so we were playing

00:01:43.236 --> 00:01:45.838
in the pool, and he just, like,
took it way too seriously --

00:01:45.838 --> 00:01:47.540
as did I, by the way.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:47.540 --> 00:01:50.944
And I was like kind of --
-Dunking on you and stuff?

00:01:50.944 --> 00:01:52.712
-I had the ball,
and he was guarding me,

00:01:52.712 --> 00:01:54.013
and it was a whole thing.
He actually ended up

00:01:54.013 --> 00:01:56.883
cutting my hand open a little
with his nail.

00:01:56.883 --> 00:01:57.984
[ Laughter ]

00:01:57.984 --> 00:01:59.385
'Cause he took it
a little too seriously.

00:01:59.385 --> 00:02:01.520
It's pretty much healed now,
so you can't really see it,

00:02:01.520 --> 00:02:04.757
but it was a big gash, and it
bothered me for like two weeks.

00:02:04.757 --> 00:02:06.626
-Oh, my gosh, yeah.
Plus, you probably

00:02:06.626 --> 00:02:08.961
had pool hands, too,
for being in there, yeah.

00:02:08.961 --> 00:02:10.696
I saw you last night
at the Met Gala.

00:02:10.696 --> 00:02:12.865
-Yeah.
-And you looked stunning.

00:02:12.865 --> 00:02:14.467
-Thank you.
-Look at this.

00:02:14.533 --> 00:02:17.070
-Thank you.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:02:17.070 --> 00:02:19.772
-I mean, holy moly.
-Thank you.

00:02:19.772 --> 00:02:22.107
[ Cheers and applause
continues ]

00:02:22.107 --> 00:02:26.078
-How long does it take you
to get ready for these events?

00:02:26.078 --> 00:02:28.414
-That took
probably like two hours.

00:02:28.414 --> 00:02:30.082
-Yeah.
-Which --

00:02:30.082 --> 00:02:31.584
And it was, like, calm.
-Yeah.

00:02:31.584 --> 00:02:32.985
-Like, I wasn't --
Usually, I feel like

00:02:32.985 --> 00:02:35.021
I'm a little more nervous...
-Yeah.

00:02:35.021 --> 00:02:37.056
-...most years,
and I was actually really just,

00:02:37.056 --> 00:02:38.891
like, calm.
I told myself before, I'm like,

00:02:38.891 --> 00:02:41.560
"Why stress yourself out
with unnecessary nerves?

00:02:41.560 --> 00:02:44.730
Just, like, be chill."
-I felt safe with all the COVID.

00:02:44.730 --> 00:02:46.165
I think they did a good job
with it.

00:02:46.165 --> 00:02:47.666
-Yeah, I felt like they did a
really good job with everything.

00:02:47.666 --> 00:02:50.035
You were the first person I saw
when I walked in with my mom.

00:02:50.035 --> 00:02:51.704
-It's almost like I greeted
everyone on their way in.

00:02:51.704 --> 00:02:52.905
-Well, by the way...
-They were like,

00:02:52.905 --> 00:02:54.640
"Get to your table, Mr. Fallon."
[ Laughter ]

00:02:54.640 --> 00:02:56.309
-That's exactly
what my mom does.

00:02:56.309 --> 00:02:58.444
She'll stand at the top --
every year, she stands

00:02:58.444 --> 00:03:00.246
at the top of the carpet,
and security's like,

00:03:00.246 --> 00:03:02.649
"Come on, ma'am. You have
to kind of go inside now.

00:03:02.649 --> 00:03:04.150
You can't just stand here
and wait around."

00:03:04.150 --> 00:03:05.718
And she's like, "Absolutely not.

00:03:05.718 --> 00:03:07.820
I'm standing here and watching
all my daughters walk in."

00:03:07.820 --> 00:03:10.657
And she's just so cute,
and she takes videos of us.

00:03:10.657 --> 00:03:13.292
The smile on her face
is just everything.

00:03:13.292 --> 00:03:14.626
-She's the greatest.
-She's the best.

00:03:14.626 --> 00:03:16.729
-Yeah, I loved seeing her,
as well.

00:03:16.729 --> 00:03:19.031
You're stepping into a different
role in the fashion world.

00:03:19.031 --> 00:03:23.202
You're the creative director
of this brand, Forward.

00:03:23.202 --> 00:03:25.404
How do you --
When did you start that,

00:03:25.404 --> 00:03:27.407
and what does that entail?
What is that?

00:03:27.407 --> 00:03:31.243
-So, Forward is a luxury
online retailer, so they --

00:03:31.243 --> 00:03:34.847
Yeah, they sell a bunch of,
like, luxury brands online,

00:03:34.847 --> 00:03:38.084
and it's really cool.
I think, for a long time,

00:03:38.084 --> 00:03:40.886
I was obviously in one space
of the fashion industry,

00:03:40.886 --> 00:03:43.957
and so it's cool
to kind of take a role

00:03:43.957 --> 00:03:45.959
in a more creative way,
personally.

00:03:45.959 --> 00:03:48.527
So it's cool.
-Like, you do, like, a --

00:03:48.527 --> 00:03:51.597
like, choosing what goes --
-Yeah, so I get to choose, like,

00:03:51.597 --> 00:03:55.868
what is featured on the website,
and I actually had my first day

00:03:55.868 --> 00:03:58.203
on the job the other day
here in New York,

00:03:58.203 --> 00:04:00.640
and I got to, like,
go around to a couple designers

00:04:00.640 --> 00:04:01.974
and pick things for the website,

00:04:01.974 --> 00:04:03.275
so it's actually
really, really fun.

00:04:03.275 --> 00:04:05.477
I get to, you know,
shop a little, so...

00:04:05.477 --> 00:04:07.313
-You get to influence
a lot of people.

00:04:07.313 --> 00:04:11.017
There was a giant headline
because you had

00:04:11.017 --> 00:04:13.352
the fashion world
going crazy because --

00:04:13.352 --> 00:04:15.954
-Oh, no.
-Oh, very controversial.

00:04:15.954 --> 00:04:18.057
You wore Crocs.
-Oh, my gosh.

00:04:18.124 --> 00:04:20.493 line:0%
-You wore Crocs.
[ Laughter and applause ]

00:04:20.493 --> 00:04:24.163 line:0%
-I -- [ Laughs ]
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:04:24.163 --> 00:04:27.266 line:0%
-Now the audience is
on your side here, but what --

00:04:27.266 --> 00:04:29.635
-Yeah, yeah. Right.
-But I've never worn Crocs.

00:04:29.635 --> 00:04:32.871
Questlove swears by Crocs.
-They're the best.

00:04:32.871 --> 00:04:36.075
-Questlove is always, like,
"Dude, I lived long enough,

00:04:36.075 --> 00:04:38.077
tried every sneaker,
shoe there is.

00:04:38.077 --> 00:04:40.513
Crocs is my jam."
-Are you wearing it right now?

00:04:40.513 --> 00:04:42.982
-Yes, I am.
-Nice. Oh, I'm jealous.

00:04:42.982 --> 00:04:44.250
[ Cheers and applause ]
I'm jealous.

00:04:44.250 --> 00:04:46.885
-You're jealous? Yeah?
Are they that comfortable?

00:04:46.885 --> 00:04:48.321
I don't know what --
-They are.

00:04:48.321 --> 00:04:51.424
I actually -- I saw those, like,
just on some, like,

00:04:51.424 --> 00:04:53.626
Instagram inspiration, like,
fashion page,

00:04:53.626 --> 00:04:54.993
and I was like, "Wait.
These are actually cute,"

00:04:54.993 --> 00:04:57.363
so I went out of my way
to buy those specific Crocs

00:04:57.363 --> 00:05:01.800
but was not expecting to be
photographed that day, clearly.

00:05:01.800 --> 00:05:03.503
I'm really, really comfortable.

00:05:03.503 --> 00:05:04.770
-Yeah, that's a comfy outfit.
Yeah.

00:05:04.770 --> 00:05:06.639
-They're great.
I am not ashamed whatsoever.

00:05:06.639 --> 00:05:08.106
-So, something like this
could maybe end up

00:05:08.106 --> 00:05:09.675
on Forward if you feel.
-Yeah.

00:05:09.675 --> 00:05:11.077
-Okay, yeah.
-Yeah, as Forward's

00:05:11.077 --> 00:05:12.911
creative director, I approve.
-Right?

00:05:12.911 --> 00:05:14.580
-I approve.
-I will tell you what

00:05:14.580 --> 00:05:16.682
I approve of, and that is...
-Oh, my gosh.

00:05:16.682 --> 00:05:19.518
[ Cheers and applause ]
-Whoo, doggie!

00:05:19.518 --> 00:05:21.587 line:0%
-Yes.
-Let me tell you something.

00:05:21.587 --> 00:05:23.422 line:0%
It's delicious.
-Thank you.

00:05:23.422 --> 00:05:25.357 line:0%
-What made you get
into the spirits business?

00:05:25.357 --> 00:05:28.494 line:0%
-So, actually, my parents
were longtime lovers.

00:05:28.494 --> 00:05:31.263
I grew up hearing about it
around the house all the time.

00:05:31.263 --> 00:05:34.901
And so, kind of as I've got
older and, you know, drank,

00:05:34.901 --> 00:05:38.036
I was always a tequila lover.
It's always my go-to drink,

00:05:38.036 --> 00:05:40.539
and me and my friends,
it's all we drink.

00:05:40.539 --> 00:05:43.909
And, you know, I kind of --
was kind of the friend

00:05:43.909 --> 00:05:46.346
that was always trying
to find new tequilas,

00:05:46.346 --> 00:05:47.813
tequilas that not everyone
might know

00:05:47.813 --> 00:05:50.583
and just loved always
experimenting with it

00:05:50.583 --> 00:05:52.785
until, you know, I kind of got
to a place where,

00:05:52.785 --> 00:05:54.119
although I found
a lot of tequilas

00:05:54.119 --> 00:05:56.355
that I really, really loved,
I didn't really see one

00:05:56.355 --> 00:05:58.324
for my generation,
something that was, like,

00:05:58.324 --> 00:06:00.593
approachable
and aesthetically pleasing

00:06:00.593 --> 00:06:03.128
and socially aware
and gender neutral,

00:06:03.128 --> 00:06:04.497
and there's, like,
so many things that I just

00:06:04.497 --> 00:06:06.465
didn't really see
for my generation.

00:06:06.465 --> 00:06:07.900
And so when I kind of
dove into it

00:06:07.900 --> 00:06:09.735
and did a little bit
of research,

00:06:09.735 --> 00:06:11.070
I found that
it was predominantly

00:06:11.070 --> 00:06:13.306
a male-dominated space,
and that kind of,

00:06:13.306 --> 00:06:16.409
coming from a really strong
female family,

00:06:16.409 --> 00:06:18.511
it just drove me even harder
into the space

00:06:18.511 --> 00:06:20.713
and made me really excited
to get into it.

00:06:20.713 --> 00:06:23.649
And then, even diving
even further into it,

00:06:23.649 --> 00:06:28.220
I found that, you know,
not a lot of brands that I saw

00:06:28.220 --> 00:06:30.522
were as, you know,
friendly to the planet

00:06:30.522 --> 00:06:33.025
as they could possibly be,
so I took that upon myself

00:06:33.025 --> 00:06:36.329
to make that a really big part
of us as a brand.

00:06:36.329 --> 00:06:38.163
-Good for you.
-So we've actually partnered

00:06:38.163 --> 00:06:41.434
with 1% for the Planet
to donate 1% of our revenue

00:06:41.434 --> 00:06:43.736
to planet-saving initiatives,
and then, along with that --

00:06:43.736 --> 00:06:47.039
[ Cheers and applause ]
Yeah, it's cool.

00:06:47.039 --> 00:06:48.874
Along with that --
Sorry, this is a mouthful.

00:06:48.874 --> 00:06:50.243
Oh, my God.
-Yeah, go for it.

00:06:50.243 --> 00:06:52.711
I like it.
-We actually --

00:06:52.711 --> 00:06:55.314
at our distillery, which I was
just at the other day,

00:06:55.314 --> 00:06:58.918
we found a way to take
the agave waste --

00:06:58.918 --> 00:07:01.220
the agave fibers
and the water waste

00:07:01.220 --> 00:07:04.657
and build the sustainable brick
that we are actually donating

00:07:04.657 --> 00:07:08.160
back to the community
of Jalisco, so it's,

00:07:08.160 --> 00:07:10.529
along with saving the planet,
it's important to us

00:07:10.529 --> 00:07:12.564
to be friendly to the community,
as well.

00:07:12.564 --> 00:07:13.699
-I thought you were gonna say
you're gonna turn them

00:07:13.699 --> 00:07:15.668
into Crocs.
-No, no.

00:07:15.668 --> 00:07:17.970
We're donating them
and building homes for people

00:07:17.970 --> 00:07:19.205
that need homes, and there's
a half-built hospital.

00:07:19.205 --> 00:07:20.973
-Actual bricks?
-Yeah, but they're all

00:07:20.973 --> 00:07:23.041
sustainable.
They're made from the waste

00:07:23.041 --> 00:07:25.277
that comes from making tequila.
-Congrats, buddy.

00:07:25.277 --> 00:07:28.047
We love you and your family,
and they've said some things

00:07:28.047 --> 00:07:30.449
over the years that have
become iconic quotes.

00:07:30.449 --> 00:07:32.185
-Okay.
-And so we wanted to put

00:07:32.185 --> 00:07:35.388
your knowledge to the test in
a new game called "Who Said It?"

00:07:35.388 --> 00:07:36.922
-Perfect.

00:07:36.922 --> 00:07:39.124
-♪ Who said it? ♪

00:07:39.124 --> 00:07:42.094
♪ Tell me who said it, yeah ♪

00:07:42.094 --> 00:07:44.363
-Why am I nervous?

00:07:44.363 --> 00:07:47.766
-How it works is I'm going to
read you real quotes that you;

00:07:47.766 --> 00:07:49.836
your sisters; or your mom, Kris,
have said,

00:07:49.836 --> 00:07:51.637
and you have to tell me
who said it.

00:07:51.637 --> 00:07:52.805
-Okay.
-Here we go.

00:07:52.805 --> 00:07:54.539
-I feel like I'm not going to be
good at this.

00:07:54.539 --> 00:07:56.308
-First up, here we go.

00:07:56.308 --> 00:07:59.211 line:0%
Who said, "Would you stop taking
pictures of yourself?

00:07:59.211 --> 00:08:01.613 line:0%
Your sister's going to jail."
[ Laughter ]

00:08:01.613 --> 00:08:04.250 line:0%
-That is actually one
of my favorite moments,

00:08:04.250 --> 00:08:05.918 line:0%
so I know exactly who this is.

00:08:05.918 --> 00:08:08.187 line:0%
It was my mom.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:08:08.187 --> 00:08:11.057 line:0%
[ Rimshot ]

00:08:11.057 --> 00:08:12.758 line:0%
-Let's see who said it.
Here we go.

00:08:12.758 --> 00:08:14.894 line:0%
-Kim, would you stop taking
pictures of yourself?

00:08:14.894 --> 00:08:16.261 line:0%
Your sister's going to jail.

00:08:16.261 --> 00:08:17.930 line:0%
[ Laughter ]
[ Ding! ]

00:08:17.930 --> 00:08:20.199 line:0%
[ Rimshot ]

00:08:20.199 --> 00:08:25.605 line:0%
-Who said, "Honey, would you put
a bumper sticker on a Bentley?"

00:08:25.605 --> 00:08:28.574 line:0%
-Kim. That was her thing
she would always say.

00:08:28.574 --> 00:08:29.975 line:0%
-Let's see who said it.

00:08:29.975 --> 00:08:33.712 line:0%
-Honey, would you put a bumper
sticker on a Bentley?

00:08:33.712 --> 00:08:37.183 line:0%
-Yeah, that's exactly right.
Good, you got two.

00:08:37.183 --> 00:08:38.784 line:0%
[ Applause ]

00:08:38.784 --> 00:08:40.819 line:0%
This one might be
my favorite one.

00:08:40.819 --> 00:08:44.190 line:0%
Who said, "You're cute jeans."

00:08:44.190 --> 00:08:46.892 line:0%
-Me.
[ Laughter ]

00:08:46.892 --> 00:08:48.827 line:0%
-I think you're right.
Let's see who said it.

00:08:48.827 --> 00:08:50.096 line:0%
-Those are cute jeans.

00:08:51.329 --> 00:08:55.867 line:0%
[ Laughter and applause ]

00:08:55.867 --> 00:08:57.770 line:0%
-I don't even know,
like, what that was.

00:08:57.770 --> 00:08:59.305 line:0%
-Dude, that was just
saying to your mom

00:08:59.305 --> 00:09:00.406 line:0%
that you love her, yeah.
-Exactly.

00:09:00.406 --> 00:09:02.274 line:0%
-She's cute.
"Those are cute jeans."

00:09:02.274 --> 00:09:03.542 line:0%
"Yeah, you're cute jeans."
-Exactly.

00:09:03.542 --> 00:09:05.278 line:0%
[ Laughter ]

00:09:05.278 --> 00:09:07.546 line:0%
-I love that.
That's my favorite quote.

00:09:07.546 --> 00:09:10.716 line:0%
Who said, "But just knowing
you're a badass bitch

00:09:10.716 --> 00:09:15.854 line:0%
is just all what life is about."

00:09:15.854 --> 00:09:18.057 line:0%
-I really don't know this one.
Oh, no.

00:09:18.057 --> 00:09:19.692 line:0%
-"Just knowing
you're a badass bitch

00:09:19.692 --> 00:09:21.293 line:0%
is just all what life is about."

